| 001-434 explicitly awaited queued files (hearsay-tools/squeal#14) | planned (triaged 2026-10-16; surface to the human before scheduling implementation) | [Issue #14](https://github.com/hearsay-tools/squeal/issues/14), labeled `friction` (agent-reported). Aligned with vision principles 3 and 4: reduce blocking and return relevant evidence sooner. Not a duplicate: 005-11/005 D2 deliberately made named waits read-only (human, 2026-10-13); 001-381 concerns splitting an already-running backlog tier for an edit. New evidence: a real-daemon probe on 0.1.199 and 0.1.200 holds A running, starts a named wait for Z, releases A, then observes unrelated queued B start while Z remains queued. Two one-second waits exit 3; Z later passes; a repeated wait runs nothing. Reproduction commands, raw logs and limits: `specifications/001-core-loop/research/issue-14-named-wait.md` and `research/probes/issue-14/README.md` beneath that spec. Original Cezarion session and several-minute latency not replayed. 001 D5 owns scheduling; coordinate any CLI contract change with 005 D2. | Human decides whether to revisit the no-priority policy, automatic versus explicit promotion, priority/fairness and binding constraints. Only after approval: regression for selected queued files ahead of unrelated queued work, running tests finish normally, unrelated work remains queued, current-key results reused, and CLI distinguishes running, promoted and constraint-blocked states. No implementation authorized by this triage. |
